Here is some sample JavaScript code which implements low transportation route planning algorithm using decision making structures like `if` / `else` statement and `switch` / `case`:

 ```javascript 
// Define input parameters
const origin = { lat: 48.8567, lng: 2.2973 }; // Origin location
const destination = { lat: 48.8510, lng: 2.3020 }; // Destination location
const biases = ["fastest", "shortest"]; // List of routing preferences (e.g., fastest, shortest)

function calculateLowTransportRoute(origin, destination, biases) {
  let route;

  // Loop through each bias preference and attempt to find a valid route
  for (let i = 0; i < biases.length; i++) {
    const currentBias = biases[i];

    // Determine whether we can use public transit for this preference
    if (currentBias === "public_transit") {
      if (canUsePublicTransit(origin, destination)) {
        route = getPublicTransitRoute(origin, destination);
        break;
      } else {
        continue; // Try next preference if cannot use pubic transit
      }
    }

    // Determine whether we can use biking for this preference
    if (currentBias === "biking") {
      if (canUseBiking(origin, destination)) {
        route = getBikeRoute(origin, destination);
        break;
      } else {
        continue; // Try next preference if cannot use biking
      }
    }

    // Determine whether we can use driving for this preference
    if (currentBias === "driving") {
      if (canUseDriving(origin, destination)) {
        route = getDriveRoute(origin, destination);
        break;
      } else {
        continue; // Try next preference if cannot use driving
      }
    }
  }

  return route;
}
``` 

In this implementation, we define two locations (`origin` and `destination`) along with a list of routing preferences represented by strings in the `biases` array. The function loops through each element in the `biases` array and attempts to find a valid route according to the specified preference. For instance, if one of the preferences is `"public_transit"`, the function checks whether it is possible to take public transit between the given origination and destination points. If so, it uses the `getPublicTransitRoute()` method to compute the optimal path. Similarly, if another preference is `"biking"`, the function calls the `canUseBiking()` and `getBikeRoute()` methods to determine if cycling is feasible and obtain the best cycle track. Finally, if all other options fail, the function returns null indicating no viable routes could be found.

